 1.1 The set $D=U=\{0,1\}$ is Borel and nonempty. The function $\mu_0(A)=\tfrac12\mathbf1_{\{0\in A\}}+\tfrac12\mathbf1_{\{1\in A\}}$ is countably additive: in a disjoint sequence of Borel sets each of the two points occurs at most once, so the sum of assigned masses equals the mass of the union. Also $\mu_0(\mathbb R)=1$ and $\mu_0(D)=1$, making it a prior on the stated domain by [F8]. The identity $F:D\to\mathbb R$ is measurable because every subset of finite $D$ is Borel, and it satisfies the forward-map interface [F9]. [F8, F9, given, construct, algebra]
 
+1.2 For any $u\in D$ and $y\in\mathbb R$, put $a:=2\pi$; since $\log a$ occurs in the real-valued potential, [F15] gives $a>0$. Put $b:=\tfrac12(y-u)^2$ and $c:=\tfrac12\log a$. By [F15], $\exp(2c)=a$. By [F16], $\exp(c)^2=\exp(2c)=a$; by [F18], $\exp(c)>0$. Thus uniqueness of the nonnegative square root [F17] gives $\exp(c)=\sqrt{2\pi}$, and [F18] gives $\exp(-c)=1/\sqrt{2\pi}$. Applying [F16] again, $e^{-\Phi(u;y)}=\exp(-(b+c))=\exp(-b)\exp(-c)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\!\left(-\frac{(y-u)^2}{2}\right)=L(u;y)$. The last expression is the displayed Gaussian density, so the likelihood and potential agree pointwise. [F15, F16, F17, F18, given, algebra]
